<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>House Price Prediction &amp; Market Analytics - Project Report</title><style>body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;line-height:1.55;margin:50px;color:#222}h1{color:#17365d}h2{color:#1f4e79;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:5px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:8px}img{max-width:100%;border:1px solid #ddd}</style></head><body><h1>House Price Prediction &amp; Market Analytics</h1><p><b>Internship Project Report</b></p><h2>1. Executive Summary</h2><p>This project combines data analytics and machine learning to understand housing-price patterns and provide an estimated median house value for a selected housing profile. The application presents KPIs, market insights, model drivers, model performance, risks, opportunities and recommended actions.</p><h2>2. Problem Statement</h2><p>Housing values vary according to demographic, geographic and housing characteristics. The objective is to transform these variables into understandable insights and build a regression model that can provide a supporting estimate of median house value.</p><h2>3. Objectives</h2><ol><li>Prepare and inspect housing data.</li><li>Analyze price distributions and relationships.</li><li>Identify important price drivers.</li><li>Build and evaluate a regression model.</li><li>Provide an interactive prediction interface.</li><li>Translate findings into business actions.</li></ol><h2>4. Dataset</h2><p>The application uses the California Housing dataset distributed through scikit-learn. It contains housing and demographic variables such as median income, house age, average rooms, average bedrooms, population, average occupancy, latitude and longitude, with median house value as the target.</p><p>Dataset documentation: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.datasets.fetch_california_housing.html</p><h2>5. Technology Stack</h2><p>Python, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Scikit-learn and Streamlit.</p><h2>6. Analytics Methodology</h2><p><b>Data → Exploration → KPIs → Drivers → ML Model → Evaluation → Prediction → Business Action</b></p><p>The application trains a Random Forest Regressor using an 80/20 train-test split with a fixed random seed. The dashboard includes distribution analysis, correlation analysis, geographic context, income-band value trends, market segments, feature importance and actual-vs-predicted evaluation.</p><h2>7. Key KPIs</h2><ul><li>Number of housing observations</li><li>Average house value</li><li>Median house value</li><li>Mean Absolute Error (MAE)</li><li>Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)</li><li>R² score</li></ul><h2>8. Business Insights</h2><p>Feature importance identifies the variables contributing most strongly to model predictions. Correlation analysis provides an additional view of individual relationships. The income-band trend helps communicate how average house value changes across income groups.</p><h2>9. Model Evaluation</h2><p>MAE measures average absolute error, RMSE places more emphasis on larger errors, and R² measures the proportion of test-set variation explained by the model. The application displays all three metrics and an actual-vs-predicted plot.</p><h2>10. Risk Analysis</h2><ul><li>Historical data may not represent current market conditions.</li><li>Unusual properties can produce less reliable estimates.</li><li>Correlation does not prove causation.</li><li>The model should not replace professional property valuation.</li></ul><h2>11. Opportunities</h2><ul><li>Use the model for preliminary valuation screening.</li><li>Identify high-value market segments.</li><li>Investigate the strongest price drivers.</li><li>Add newer local and time-based datasets.</li><li>Extend geographic analysis with richer location data.</li></ul><h2>12. Recommended Actions</h2><ol><li>Use model estimates as an initial screening signal.</li><li>Manually review properties with unusual input profiles.</li><li>Monitor model performance as new data becomes available.</li><li>Retrain the model with newer market data.</li><li>Validate estimates with local market information before operational decisions.</li></ol><h2>13. Application Visual</h2><p>The following generated visual represents the structure and presentation of the Streamlit dashboard. It is included as documentation of the application layout; the live interface is produced by <code>house_price_app.py</code>.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LRBishnoi/ibmBobProject/main/dashboard_preview.svg" alt="Dashboard visual overview"></p><h2>14. Conclusion</h2><p>The project demonstrates a decision-oriented data analytics workflow that converts housing data into KPIs, insights, predictions and recommended actions. It is designed as an educational/internship decision-support application rather than a professional valuation service.</p><h2>15. How to Run</h2><pre>pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run house_price_app.py</pre></body></html>